Ladybird runs every extension storage operation in order on one worker,
and replaces a versioned JSON file atomically before publishing a
change. Quota counts each key’s UTF-8 bytes plus its value’s JSON, and a
write that leaves a value unchanged reports no change for it.

Chromium counts quota the same way, in SettingsStorageQuotaEnforcer::
Allocate(), from JSON written by base::WriteJson() (whose escapes
and number formats Ladybird’s quota count follows exactly),
and LeveldbValueStore::Set() drops unchanged values in AddToBatch().
Ladybird matches those rules, with a JSON file in place of LevelDB.

Gecko stores structured clones in an IndexedDB transaction,
in ExtensionStorageIDB.set(), and aborts it when a put fails;
so a failed write is atomic there too, but every supplied key is
reported. WebKit runs WebExtensionStorageSQLiteStore::setKeyedData() on
a storage queue and completes on the main queue, just as Ladybird does;
but it writes SQLite rows one at a time, so a failure partway leaves
the earlier keys written.
